fc8a0ac3 6qzoi6V6dcFi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 102:82a7616e63686f727391da00492f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f70726f7669646572732f636c617564655f636f64652e676f3a3136373a312d3339373a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d107c2 This is the Query method of ClaudeCodeProvider, which annotates a rooted observation graph with LLM-generated explanations. It first walks the graph via BFS from the root, collecting every vertex (aborting early if the context is cancelled or a vertex lookup fails, surfacing that via innerErr), then fetches all edge keys and resolves each into a full edge. Under the workspace mutex it builds a batch request: for each vertex it computes an ObservationKey from the vertex hash and query; on a cache hit it writes the cached answer directly onto the graph as the 'observation-answer' vertex attribute and skips the LLM; otherwise it assigns a short sequential id like 's0', records the id-to-hash mapping in verteximap, and appends the vertex source text to the batch input. Edges are handled analogously with 'c0'-style ids, a derived edge hash key ('edge:<src>:<dst>'), a cache check that sets the edge attribute, and a human-readable '<src> loads <dst>' description. If every item was served from cache it returns nil without calling the LLM. Otherwise it constructs a strict system prompt (no code changes, factual observations, raw JSON, one output entry per input entry, self-contained answers), marshals the batch input and an empty BatchObservationSchema sample to communicate the output shape, and invokes QueryClaudeCode with 'high' effort expecting a *common.BatchObservationSchema back. On response, again under the mutex, it maps each returned observation id back through verteximap/edgeimap; unknown ids record a ProviderOutputError (only the first error is kept) and are skipped. For valid entries it rewrites the id to the stable cache-key string, resolves the vertex (or both edge endpoints) to build FileRange anchors, wraps the result in a COGObservation, sets the 'observation-answer' attribute on the vertex or edge, and persists it into the workspace cache via SetObservationLocked. It returns the first response-mapping error encountered, or nil on full success.
fc8a0ac3 As-elg1b43Ni5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 176: This connection states that the module or file identified by hash ux3KdU1l9A_53MzYA1mpeFWSuUNi9gDH_2DInzsKsto loads (depends on / imports) the one identified by Pl9DfG8h_AUaNES70hMhRBMBmKjKgclhXo5i4C2EvPc. In the observation graph this is a directed edge from the loading source vertex to the loaded target vertex, meaning the source's behavior relies on code defined in the target; explanations of the source should account for functionality it pulls in from the target.
fc8a0ac3 lu-hybSPvkRi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 98:82a7616e63686f727391da00452f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f74657374732f636f675f6c6f61645f66696c655f746573742e676f3a313a312d38353a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10593 Go test file (package tests_test) exercising the cog workspace API with four tests. TestCOGLoadFilesDeduplicatesConcurrentLoads builds a slice of 32 identical relative paths to fixtures/golang/baseproj/simple.go, calls c.LoadFiles once, asserts no error and that 32 results are returned, then asserts every returned pointer is the Same (pointer-identical) as the first — proving concurrent/duplicate loads of one path are deduplicated into a single shared file object. TestCOGLoadFileClearsInflightAfterError first calls LoadFile on a missing main.go in a temp workspace and expects an error, then writes the file and calls LoadFile again expecting success — verifying a failed load does not leave a stale inflight/cached error entry that would poison retries. TestCOGPersistReturns runs c.Persist() in a goroutine sending its error to a buffered channel and uses select with a 1-second time.After timeout to assert Persist returns promptly without error (guards against deadlock/hang). TestOpenCOGLoadsPersistedCache creates an observation (common.PrimaryHash key, NewObservationSchema, wrapped via cog.NewCOGObservation with empty FileRange slice), inserts it into c.ObservationCache, persists, then reopens the workspace with cog.OpenWorkspace and asserts the reloaded ObservationCache entry equals the original — verifying persistence round-trips the observation cache. All assertions use testify assert (no require).
fc8a0ac3 mALymw9IPD9i5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 99:82a7616e63686f727391da00462f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f74657374732f636f675f6c6f61645f66696c655f746573742e676f3a37313a312d38343a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10315 Test verifying the observation-cache persistence round-trip in the cog package. It creates a temp workspace, computes h := common.PrimaryHash("persisted-observation"), builds an ObservationSchema whose ID is h.String() with answer "persisted answer", and wraps it in a COGObservation via cog.NewCOGObservation with an empty []*common.FileRange. It then constructs a workspace with cog.NewWorkspace, directly inserts the observation into the exported ObservationCache map keyed by h, and asserts c.Persist() succeeds (writing cache state to disk). Finally it calls cog.OpenWorkspace on the same directory and asserts the freshly loaded workspace's ObservationCache[h] deep-equals the original COGObservation, proving OpenWorkspace deserializes previously persisted cache entries faithfully.
fc8a0ac3 nua7fCXzbPpi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 171: The test suite loads/depends on the target code: TestOpenCOGLoadsPersistedCache invokes cog.OpenWorkspace after a Persist to read back the on-disk observation cache. The connection reflects that the test calls into the workspace open/load path — Persist writes ObservationCache entries (keyed by common.PrimaryHash) to the workspace directory, and OpenWorkspace loads that persisted state into a new workspace instance, which the test asserts via equality of the cached COGObservation. Thus the source's correctness check directly exercises the target's deserialization behavior.
fc8a0ac3 463sv4NO82Bi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 89:82a7616e63686f727391da003c2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f7475692f7374617475732e676f3a313a312d3133393a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10560 Go package `tui` implementing a context-scoped status reporting system for a terminal overlay. A `StatusProvider` wraps an `*Overlay` and guards a shared `*Timer` plus a `tasks` string stack with a `sync.Mutex`. `WithStatusProvider` stores a new provider in a context under a private typed key (`statusContextKeyType`) to avoid key collisions; `GetStatusProvider` retrieves it via a type assertion. The package-level helpers `ReportStatus` and `PushStatusTask` are no-op safe: if no provider is in the context they return immediately (or return an empty cleanup func). `PushTask` lazily creates and starts the timer on the first task, appends the status to the stack, shows the overlay, and renders the status; it returns a cleanup closure protected by `sync.Once` so calling it multiple times only pops once. `popTask` removes the top task; if tasks remain it re-renders the new top status (restoring the previous status, LIFO semantics), otherwise it stops and clears the timer and hides the overlay. Note the mutex is released before calling `p.report`/`p.overlay` methods, keeping I/O outside the critical section. `report` builds a shimmer-decorated status line ('⚓ captn' + status, plus the timer section when non-nil) and pushes it to the overlay via `SetStatus`. Methods also tolerate nil receivers (`if p == nil`), making the whole API safe to call unconditionally.
fc8a0ac3 1Hpf0JEp5Z1i5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 91:82a7616e63686f727391da003e2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f7475692f7374617475732e676f3a3131343a312d3132343a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10258 `ReportStatus` method on `*StatusProvider`: updates the displayed status text without modifying the task stack. It first guards against a nil receiver (no-op if `p == nil`), then briefly acquires the mutex only to snapshot the current `*Timer` pointer, releases the lock, and calls the unexported `report(status, timer)` which renders the status (with the timer section if one is running) to the overlay. Taking the timer snapshot under the lock ensures a consistent read while keeping the rendering call outside the critical section; the `StatusType` parameter is accepted but not used in rendering.
