fc8a0ac3 z_kK0CS-kMxi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 94:82a7616e63686f727391da00412f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6d63702f6578706c61696e5f746f6f6c2e676f3a313a312d39343a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10483 This file defines an MCP 'explain' tool in package mcp. ExplainInput carries a workdir-relative filePath and a code snippet; ExplainOutput carries the explanation text and operation duration in milliseconds, constructed via NewExplainOutput. ExplainTool implements a Tool interface (verified by the `var explainTool Tool[ExplainInput, ExplainOutput]` assertion) exposing Name() 'explain', DisplayName(), and Description(). Its Call method does the real work: it records a start time, resolves the current working directory, opens a COG workspace over it with cog.OpenWorkspace, constructs a Claude Code LLM provider, locates the snippet in the file via g.SearchSnippet (yielding an observation graph and a start node), then runs og.QueryWithDepth with queries.NewExplainBehaviorQuery() at depth 1 to produce the explanation. Every failure path returns a zero-valued output plus a wrapped error. After success it fires a goroutine to g.Persist() the graph asynchronously (errors merely printed, noted as a TODO because prints clash with claude stdout), computes elapsed milliseconds, and returns the explanation and duration with a nil *mcp.CallToolResult.
fc8a0ac3 pCYP6LNo7QRi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 95:82a7616e63686f727391da00422f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6d63702f6578706c61696e5f746f6f6c2e676f3a34373a312d39313a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10431 Call is the ExplainTool's MCP handler. Flow: (1) build a zero-value ExplainOutput used on all error returns; (2) capture time.Now() for duration measurement; (3) os.Getwd() to find the working directory, wrapping failure as 'invalid CWD'; (4) cog.OpenWorkspace(cwd) to open the code observation graph workspace; (5) create a Claude Code provider via providers.NewClaudeCodeProvider(); (6) g.SearchSnippet(ctx, input.FilePath, input.Snippet) to build an ObservationGraph and identify the start node for the requested snippet, wrapping errors as 'failed to query snippet'; (7) og.QueryWithDepth(ctx, g, prov, start, queries.NewExplainBehaviorQuery(), 1) to have the LLM explain behavior traversing one level of depth. On success it launches a background goroutine calling g.Persist(), only printing persistence failures (a TODO notes stdout collision with claude output — the write is fire-and-forget, so Call may return before persistence finishes). Finally it computes elapsed milliseconds and returns NewExplainOutput(expln, dur) with a nil CallToolResult and nil error.
fc8a0ac3 3y-CgfosHwhi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 94:82a7616e63686f727391da00412f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f636f672f776f726b73706163652e676f3a3533313a312d3637323a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10610 SearchSnippet builds a one-level ObservationGraph around a snippet of code. Steps: load the target file through the workspace (cached loader), locate the snippet's byte range with FindSnippetRange, and collect AST nodes inside that range plus the file's Module as root. It then starts an LSP client for the file's language and, for every ASTCallExpression among the in-range children, records the call symbol's position. Those positions are sent as a single batched textDocument/definition request (lsp.NewDefinitionBatchRequest with the full buffer as a TextDocumentItem). Each returned location (possibly several per range, kept for future compatibility) is converted into a ResolvedDependency linking an internal range to an external file range, with descriptive errors on failure. The external files are preloaded in bulk via LoadFiles so subsequent per-dependency LoadFile calls hit cache. For each dependency it asserts exactly one AST node exists at both the internal and external ranges (erroring otherwise), appends the external node to the child set, and records a linkage from the external node to the nearest node-of-interest ancestor of the internal node (ichains). All collected children are collapsed to their nearest node-of-interest into a set. Finally it constructs a cgraph graph keyed by NodeHasher, adds root as a vertex, adds each interest node, and wires edges: from the linked upstream node when the node appears in ichains (cross-file dependency edge), otherwise from root. Returns the ObservationGraph, the root node, and nil.
fc8a0ac3 CxUCtVsj9U1i5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 163: This is a file-load edge: the snippet-search routine, running inside the explain tool's workspace, loads another source file. During SearchSnippet, LSP definition results for call expressions in the snippet resolve to external files, and those files are fetched through wspace.LoadFiles/LoadFile (cache-backed) so their ASTs can be parsed and the referenced definition nodes located. The loaded file's matching node becomes a depth-1 vertex in the observation graph, linked by an edge to the calling node of interest in the original file.
fc8a0ac3 fGw2_syYPLFi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 162: This edge records that the same snippet-resolution pass loads an additional dependency file. When SearchSnippet batches textDocument/definition lookups for the snippet's call expressions, each resolved location may live in a different file; every such file is preloaded via wspace.LoadFiles and then re-opened from cache with LoadFile to extract exactly one AST node at the resolved range. That external node is appended to the graph and connected via ichains to the nearest node-of-interest in the source file, giving the explain query cross-file context at depth 1.
